Subject: [PATCH 0/6] watchdog: pm8916_wdt: Second generation PON (PM8998, PM6150)

The PMIC watchdog of the PON block is also found in the second
generation PON of PM8998 and later PMICs, at the same register offsets;
only the power-off reason registers moved. This series adds support for
it, and fixes two things found while bringing it up on two phones:

1. The S2 reset type is written blind at probe, while S2 may already be
   enabled by the bootloader. The PON takes a new type only while S2 is
   disabled, so the firmware's type can stay. Disable S2 around the
   write and read the type back.

2. S2 outlives a reset of the SoC. Rebooted with the watchdog open, a
   following kernel without this driver is reset every timeout seconds.
   Stop the watchdog on reboot and on unregister.

Then the PM8998 compatible (PM6150 with PM8998 as fallback), the driver
support, and the nodes in pm8998.dtsi and pm6150.dtsi, left disabled.

Testing, on hardware:

- Pixel 2 (MSM8998, PM8998), this series on next-20261118: the driver
  probes; the bootloader had left the S2 type at 0x01, patch 1 set it to
  0x07 and the read-back passed. Armed and never pinged, the PMIC reset
  the phone 20 s after arming. With USB connected the bootloader then
  stopped in its off-mode charging screen, and Android started after a
  press of the power button. Armed and then rebooted normally, the phone
  booted Android and stayed up with no further reset, and S2 read as
  disabled on the next boot (patch 2).
- POCO X3 NFC (SM7150, PM6150), on the sm7150-mainline tree (7.1-based)
  with patches 1, 2 and 4 backported unchanged: the driver probes; the
  bootloader had left the S2 type at 0x01, patch 1 set it to 0x07 and
  the read-back passed. A bite has not been tested on this phone yet.

Rebased onto next-20261119 for sending: built with LLVM W=1, checkpatch
--strict, dt_binding_check for the binding, and CHECK_DTBS on
msm8998-oneplus-cheeseburger, sm7125-xiaomi-joyeuse and
sc7180-acer-aspire1 with the new nodes enabled: no new warnings.

These patches were written with an AI coding assistant (Claude),
reviewed by me, and tested on the phones above.

